{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md | sed -n '1,160p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/assemble_readings.py | sed -n '1,190p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, re-run after D741: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5; D740, D741)\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP**, on one confirmed registered stop condition, with unresolved reader marks beside it: a Part B refusal that refuses a right answer.\n     4\t- P-v4's Claude writer was refused by S4: \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\".\n     5\t- F4 (the $108.0B Q3 guidance) never enters the total as a Q3 value. It builds F9 = growth(Q2 $96.2B, F4) = 12.27%, the guided Q2 to\n     6\t  Q3 growth, and Q4 = apply_growth(F8 $112.5B, F9) = $126.3B. The only Q3 value in the revenue sum is F8.\n     7\t- Both refusal reviewers: the request states the rule (the header's \"Shared quantities … through which every figure an outcome uses\n     8\t  for that quantity goes\"), and the answer is right.\n     9\t- Both readers and both adjudicators call the forecast supported.\n    10\t\n    11\tEvery other registered line passes:\n    12\t- Part A (104 cases, 40 mutants, 19 rehearsal checks) was green before the run.\n    13\t- The run is complete: 12 first replies, 24 readings, 6 adjudication packets, each read by two adjudicators.\n    14\t- Every case has at least one usable reply. The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R05 and R09).\n    15\t- No accepted reply carries a settled defect: no defect was upheld by both adjudicators, and no reader pair marked the same defect.\n    16\t  R07 comes closest: both readers marked F3, but under different types, and each adjudicator pair split. Across the six packets, five\n    17\t  marks were jointly rejected, nine split and none was jointly upheld (Council round 56).\n    18\t\n    19\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/`. It cost $0 at the provider: writers, readers, reviewers and adjudicators were fresh\n    20\tClaude subagents and fresh read-only Codex sessions. Adjudication packets are built by `blind_adjudicate.py`; the tally is\n    21\t`TALLY.json`.\n    22\t\n    23\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    24\t\n    25\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    26\t|---|---|---|\n    27\t| P (joint) | publish: v4 413.1, v8 382.4, both rung 2 | publish: v4 419.2, v8 388.3, both rung 2 |\n    28\t| P-v4 | **refused (S4)**: 416.6, rung 2 | publish: 416.4, rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v8 | publish: 383.6, rung 2 | publish: 388.6, rung 2 |\n    30\t| S (CY2026) | publish: 717.8, rung 4 | publish: 680.8, rung 3 |\n    31\t| O | publish: 14, rung 1 | publish: 16, rung 1 |\n    32\t| E | publish: 36, rung 3 | publish: 36, rung 3 |\n    33\t\n    34\t- 12 of 12 were schema-valid.\n    35\t- 11 of 12 passed every code check.\n    36\t- D741's three rulings were applied:\n    37\t  - S applies a stated deduction from the total in both replies (Claude 3% non-data-center share; Codex 92% data-center share).\n    38\t  - No v8 reference carries a past quarter's share (the S5 [D741] check refused none).\n    39\t  - D740's \"from … to …\" fix is exercised by E's Claude writer, who copied the CPU market figures without a range refusal (E's Codex\n    40\t    writer did not use them).\n    41\t\n    42\t## 2. The refusal: mechanism and census\n    43\t\n    44\t- **The mechanism.** S4's bypass walk starts at each outcome and follows `inputs`. It records every figure whose key is a listed shared\n    45\t  quantity, and it descends through every non-forecast figure, rates included. F9's unit is %, yet the walk descended into F9's inputs\n    46\t  and reached F4. A rate computed from a quarter's guidance is another quantity. The quarter's value in the calculation is still F8\n    47\t  alone.\n    48\t- **The ruling it contradicts.** D741 ruling 1 (Alex: \"不算错\"): a growth rate computed from guidance and used as a benchmark is not a\n    49\t  second value when one figure supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation. The question Alex answered named the deciding point:\n    50\t  \"进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个\". The system text also asks the writer to give \"a run-rate that carries the guided growth\n    51\t  forward\". The schema's benchmark sentence names \"an estimate\", and that is the narrower wording the code follows.\n    52\t- **The census, $0** (`s4_census.py`). Every stored v13s first reply from both blind tests (24) goes through the current checker and\n    53\t  through a candidate copy.\n    54\t  - **A blanket candidate, rejected by Council round 56.** It stopped the walk beneath any % or x figure\n    55\t    (`s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt`). It changed 1 of 24 and passed the 104 cases. However, it also publishes a reply that builds Q4\n    56\t    on the guided $108B through a multiple applied twice to Q2 (96.2 × (108/96.2)²) while Q3 = $112.5B stands in the sum. That is a\n    57\t    second Q3 value, and the current checker refuses it.\n    58\t  - **The narrowed candidate (round 56b).** At an `apply_growth` whose base is the quarter's forecast, or is computed from it, the walk\n    59\t    skips the rate. Every other path is walked as before.\n    60\t    - 1 of 24 changes: this refusal, which becomes publish.\n    61\t    - The round-56 counterexample on the saved reply stays refused.\n    62\t    - Results on the scratch copy: 109 of 109 cases (5 new), 43 of 43 control and mutants (2 new), 19 of 19 rehearsal.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 3. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    65\t\n    66\tEach reply had a Claude reader and a Codex reader. Six replies drew a disagreement. Two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point\n    67\tagainst the passages and the format rules (the D741 rulings included). A point counts as settled when both agree.\n    68\t\n    69\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    70\t- every \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance given as its midpoint (R06 F7, R09 F4, R10 F3). The format permits this.\n    71\t- R11 F4 (\"up to\" stands before the $3B total in e1919's quote, not before the $1B tranche).\n    72\t- R11 F12 (42 + 25 = 67 with \"more than\" kept on the source).\n    73\t\n    74\t**Unsettled: the families split** (in every split, the Codex adjudicator upholds and the Claude adjudicator rejects). They fall into\n    75\ttwo classes.\n    76\t1. **O's \"$18 billion\" (R07, R11).** e2889: \"it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year\".\n    77\t   Both O writers read it as the part of e2620's $25B balance funded by year-end, stated that reading as their assumption, and built\n    78\t   the reference as 25 − 18 = 7. The rung depends on that reading: without the deduction the balance starts at $25B, which is rung 2.\n    79\t   - **R07, \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\" on F3.** D741 ruling 2 does not settle it. The ruling names what can\n    80\t     be the market's reference (an outside figure for the member's own quantity, or a calculation that carries no past share). That is\n    81\t     a condition, not a guarantee. F3 = 25 − 18 still rests on the writer's reading that the $18B is part of the $25B and will be paid\n    82\t     by year-end, and the passages do not state that reading (Council round 56 corrected my first draft here).\n    83\t   - **R07, \"two forecasts of one quarter\" on F3 and F10.** F3 has the outcome's key but is the July balance still unfunded, a part of\n    84\t     the year-end total. The Claude adjudicator calls it the company-schedule baseline beneath the forecast, which the format allows.\n    85\t     The Codex adjudicator calls it a part labelled as the whole.\n    86\t   - **R11.** The Codex family calls F2's \"guidance to fund in Q3–Q4\" an unsupported schedule and the forecast not supported. The\n    87\t     Claude family says \"for the remainder of its fiscal year\" gives the timing and the answer states the reading.\n    88\t   - **R11 F13** also split. It attaches a period and a funded-and-unfunded scope to Bank of America's $70B figure, which e1732 says\n    89\t     was \"not specified\". F13 feeds neither the outcome nor the reference.\n    90\t   - The first test split the same way on the same passage (D740, R02).\n    91\t2. **Statements in the answer's prose that no figure carries** (R03, R09, R11). Codex upholds them as \"number misread\" or \"wrong\n    92\t   arithmetic\". Claude rejects them because those types are defined on source and computed figures. I read each one against its\n    93\t   passage:\n    94\t   - R11: \"if the $18B funding slips into FY2028 … probably keep it below $25B\" is wrong on the answer's own numbers: a full slip gives\n    95\t     25 + 7 = 32, rung 2.\n    96\t   - R09: \"$115bn, then 20% sequential growth\" is offered as reaching rung 1, but it gives 430.8 against the 431.2 edge.\n    97\t   - R09: \"both 6 GW\" drops e1041's \"up to\".\n    98\t   - R03: \"Vera Rubin is already about 20%\" states e2721's expectation as achieved.\n    99\t   - R03 \"flat second half\" is disputed: the Claude adjudicator shows a reading under which it holds.\n   100\t   None of these enters a figure, an outcome or a rung. They are what the card would show an analyst. Code does not check prose\n   101\t   (D737), and the registered defect types do not cover it.\n   102\t\n   103\t**Support.** Every forecast is supported by both adjudicators except R11 (O, Claude writer), where the families split as in class 1.\n   104\t\n   105\t## 4. What the test shows\n   106\t\n   107\t- **The request and the rulings hold.** There were no schema refusals and no unstated rule. D741's three rulings were applied by the\n   108\t  writers and are visible to code (S5) and readers.\n   109\t- **One checker rule still conflicts with a ruling.** It is S4's walk through a guidance rate applied to the forecast. A narrowed\n   110\t  exemption flips the refused case, keeps the round-56 counterexample refused, and changes no other stored decision.\n   111\t- **The reader families still split on two things.**\n   112\t  - One is a source reading: what \"$18 billion committed … for the remainder of its fiscal year\" covers.\n   113\t  - The other is whether a wrong number in prose is a defect.\n   114\t  - Neither is a registered stop, because neither was settled. Both reach the product: the first decides O's rung, and the second is\n   115\t    what an analyst reads.\n   116\t- **One draw per writer.** P's six replies are 413–419 (v4) and 382–389 (v8), all rung 2. That is no evidence about gpt-6-luna.\n     1\t\"\"\"The blind reading step of the judging-v13 paid gate (PAID-GATE.md §4): split packets.md into reader batches, list the\n     2\tdisagreements between readers A and B for a third reader, and assemble the readings file that `paid_gate.py verdict` seals.\n     3\t\n     4\tUsage:\n     5\t  python3 assemble_readings.py split <packets.md> <out folder> <batches>\n     6\t  python3 assemble_readings.py third <packets.md> <reader A json> <reader B json> <out prompt file>\n     7\t  python3 assemble_readings.py assemble <packets.md> <reader A json> <reader B json> <third json or -> <out readings json>\n     8\t\"\"\"\n     9\timport hashlib\n    10\timport json\n    11\timport re\n    12\timport sys\n    13\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    14\t\n    15\tHERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n    16\tQUESTIONS = ('usable', 'contradiction', 'internal_reference', 'deciding_gap')\n    17\t\n    18\t\n    19\tdef sections(text):\n    20\t    \"\"\"The packets in order: [(pid, section text)], each section starting at its '## P..' line.\"\"\"\n    21\t    found = list(re.finditer(r'^## (P\\d+)\\n', text, re.M))\n    22\t    return [(m.group(1), text[m.start():(found[i + 1].start() if i + 1 < len(found) else len(text))]) for i, m in enumerate(found)]\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\tdef read_json(path):\n    26\t    \"\"\"A reader's answer: the outermost JSON object in the file (fences and prose around it are ignored).\"\"\"\n    27\t    text = Path(path).read_text()\n    28\t    start, end = text.find('{'), text.rfind('}')\n    29\t    if start < 0 or end < start:\n    30\t        raise SystemExit(f'{path}: no JSON object')\n    31\t    return json.loads(text[start:end + 1])\n    32\t\n    33\t\n    34\tdef split(packets_path, out, batches):\n    35\t    text = Path(packets_path).read_text()\n    36\t    parts = sections(text)\n    37\t    brief = (HERE / 'reader-brief-v13.md').read_text()\n    38\t    out.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=False)\n    39\t    size = -(-len(parts) // batches)\n    40\t    for b in range(batches):\n    41\t        chunk = parts[b * size:(b + 1) * size]\n    42\t        if chunk:\n    43\t            (out / f'batch-{b + 1}.md').write_text(brief + '\\n\\n# Packets\\n\\n' + ''.join(s for _, s in chunk))\n    44\t            print(f'batch-{b + 1}: {\", \".join(p for p, _ in chunk)} ({sum(len(s) for _, s in chunk)} chars)')\n    45\t\n    46\t\n    47\tdef disagreements(a, b):\n    48\t    return sorted((pid, card, q) for pid in a for card in a[pid] for q in QUESTIONS\n    49\t                  if a[pid][card].get(q, {}).get('answer') != b.get(pid, {}).get(card, {}).get(q, {}).get('answer'))\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\tdef third(packets_path, a_path, b_path, out):\n    53\t    text = Path(packets_path).read_text()\n    54\t    by_pid = dict(sections(text))\n    55\t    a, b = read_json(a_path), read_json(b_path)\n    56\t    rows = disagreements(a, b)\n    57\t    brief = (HERE / 'reader-brief-v13.md').read_text()\n    58\t    lines = [brief, '\\n# Third reading\\n',\n    59\t             'Two readers disagreed on the questions listed below. For each one, read the packet and both readings, and give your own '\n    60\t             'answer (yes or no) with a ground naming an id of that packet. Return only JSON: '\n    61\t             '{\"P01\": {\"K1\": {\"usable\": {\"answer\": \"...\", \"ground\": \"...\"}}}} with exactly the listed packet, card and question keys.\\n']\n    62\t    for pid in sorted({r[0] for r in rows}):\n    63\t        lines.append(by_pid[pid])\n    64\t        for _, card, q in [r for r in rows if r[0] == pid]:\n    65\t            lines.append(f'Disagreement: {pid} {card} {q}\\n  Reader 1: {json.dumps(a[pid][card][q], ensure_ascii=False)}\\n'\n    66\t                         f'  Reader 2: {json.dumps(b[pid][card][q], ensure_ascii=False)}\\n')\n    67\t    Path(out).write_text('\\n'.join(lines))\n    68\t    print(f'{len(rows)} disagreements in {len({r[0] for r in rows})} packets')\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\tdef assemble(packets_path, a_path, b_path, third_path, out):\n    72\t    data = Path(packets_path).read_bytes()\n    73\t    a, b = read_json(a_path), read_json(b_path)\n    74\t    t = read_json(third_path) if third_path != '-' else {}\n    75\t    readings = {}\n    76\t    for pid in sorted(set(a) | set(b)):\n    77\t        readings[pid] = {}\n    78\t        for card in sorted(set(a.get(pid, {})) | set(b.get(pid, {}))):\n    79\t            entry = {'A': a.get(pid, {}).get(card, {}), 'B': b.get(pid, {}).get(card, {})}\n    80\t            if t.get(pid, {}).get(card):\n    81\t                entry['third'] = t[pid][card]\n    82\t            readings[pid][card] = entry\n    83\t    Path(out).write_text(json.dumps({'packets_sha256': hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest(), 'packets': readings}, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n    84\t    print(f'{len(readings)} packets assembled; {len(disagreements(a, b))} disagreements, {sum(len(v) for v in t.values())} cards with a third reading')\n    85\t\n    86\t\n    87\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n    88\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ''\n    89\t    if cmd == 'split' and len(sys.argv) == 5:\n    90\t        split(sys.argv[2], Path(sys.argv[3]), int(sys.argv[4]))\n    91\t    elif cmd == 'third' and len(sys.argv) == 6:\n    92\t        third(*sys.argv[2:6])\n    93\t    elif cmd == 'assemble' and len(sys.argv) == 7:\n    94\t        assemble(*sys.argv[2:7])\n    95\t    else:\n    96\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '13,42p;138,181p;384,429p'; ls poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    13\tKINDS = {'source', 'computed', 'estimate'}\n    14\tBASES = {'reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'}\n    15\tEXTERNAL = {'consensus', 'guidance', 'third_party'}\n    16\tUNITS = {'USD bn', '%', 'x'}\n    17\tARITY = {'sum': (2, None), 'product': (2, None), 'difference': (2, 2), 'ratio': (2, 2), 'share': (2, 2), 'growth': (2, 2),\n    18\t         'apply_growth': (2, 2)}\n    19\tQUALIFIERS_BEFORE = sorted(['more than', 'at least', 'up to', 'as much as', 'less than', 'over', 'about', 'approximately', 'around',\n    20\t                            'roughly', 'nearly', 'almost', 'below', 'under', 'near'], key=len, reverse=True)\n    21\tQUALIFIERS_AFTER = ['or more', 'or less']\n    22\tQUALIFIERS = ['none'] + QUALIFIERS_BEFORE + QUALIFIERS_AFTER\n    23\tSCALE = {'trillion': 1000.0, 'tn': 1000.0, 't': 1000.0, 'billion': 1.0, 'bn': 1.0, 'b': 1.0, 'million': 0.001, 'mn': 0.001, 'm': 0.001}\n    24\tLIMIT = 1e9                                              # [54] a number beyond this is refused, not computed\n    25\tMAX_RANGE_LEAVES = 12\n    26\t\n    27\t_NUM = r'(\\d[\\d,]*(?:\\.\\d+)?)'\n    28\t_CUR = r'(?:US)?\\$\\s?'\n    29\t_SC = r'(?:\\s*(trillion|billion|million|bn|tn|mn|t|b|m)\\b)?'\n    30\t_MINUS = r'([-−–]\\s?)?'\n    31\t_PCT = r'\\s*(?:%|percent)'\n    32\t_BODIES = [   # [54] the complete numeric expressions, longest forms first; a quote_number must be one of them, whole\n    33\t    ('relative', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*,?\\s*\\(?\\s*(?:plus or minus|\\+/-|±)\\s*' + _NUM + r'\\s*%\\s*\\)?'),\n    34\t    ('range', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*(?:–|—|-|to)\\s*(?:' + _CUR + r')?' + _NUM + _SC),\n    35\t    ('range', r'between\\s+' + _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s+and\\s+(?:' + _CUR + r')?' + _NUM + _SC),\n    36\t    ('percent_range', r'between\\s+' + _NUM + r'\\s*%?\\s+and\\s+' + _NUM + _PCT),\n    37\t    ('percent_range', _NUM + r'\\s*%?\\s*(?:–|—|-|to)\\s*' + _NUM + _PCT),\n    38\t    ('money', _MINUS + _CUR + _NUM + _SC),\n    39\t    ('percent', _MINUS + _NUM + _PCT),\n    40\t    ('multiple', r'(double|triple|twice)'),\n    41\t]\n    42\t_ANCHORED = [(form, re.compile(r'^' + body + r'$', re.I)) for form, body in _BODIES]\n   138\t            return None\n   139\t        quarters = set(range(lo, hi + 1))\n   140\t    return year, quarters\n   141\t\n   142\t\n   143\tdef _overlap(p1, p2):\n   144\t    \"\"\"[54] Two periods overlap: the same label, or parsed fiscal periods of one year that share a quarter.\"\"\"\n   145\t    if p1 == p2:\n   146\t        return True\n   147\t    a, b = _period(p1), _period(p2)\n   148\t    return bool(a and b and a[0] == b[0] and a[1] & b[1])\n   149\t\n   150\t\n   151\t# ---------- decoding the wire shape ----------\n   152\tFIGURE_FIELDS = ('id', 'what', 'kind', 'basis', 'value', 'low', 'high', 'unit', 'quote_number', 'qualifier', 'inputs', 'operation',\n   153\t                 'basis_text', 'rests_on')\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\tdef _text(value):\n   157\t    return value if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip() else None\n   158\t\n   159\t\n   160\tdef from_answer(data, family):\n   161\t    \"\"\"The v13s wire answer as the internal shape: `evidence_id` becomes `evidence`; the markers become each answer's `outcome` and\n   162\t    `reference`; marker errors go in `decode_errors` (S5, S6, S7). An empty string counts as absent.\"\"\"\n   163\t    errors, figures, outcome_for, reference_for = [], [], {}, {}\n   164\t    members = family['members']\n   165\t    for f in data.get('figures') or []:\n   166\t        g = {k: f[k] for k in FIGURE_FIELDS if f.get(k) is not None}\n   167\t        if f.get('evidence_id') is not None:\n   168\t            g['evidence'] = f['evidence_id']\n   169\t        for marks, store in (('outcome_for', outcome_for), ('reference_for', reference_for)):\n   170\t            listed = f.get(marks) or []\n   171\t            for v in sorted(set(listed)):\n   172\t                if listed.count(v) > 1:\n   173\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{f.get(\"id\")}: {marks} lists {v} more than once'))\n   174\t                store.setdefault(v, []).append(f.get('id'))\n   175\t        figures.append(g)\n   176\t    answers = []\n   177\t    for a in data.get('answers') or []:\n   178\t        v = a.get('view_id')\n   179\t        a2 = {k: a[k] for k in ('view_id', 'shape', 'rung', 'reasons', 'cites', 'pushes', 'theme_pushes') if a.get(k) is not None}\n   180\t        if _text(a.get('why')):\n   181\t            a2['why'] = a['why']\n   384\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: kind, basis or unit outside the closed set'))\n   385\t            continue\n   386\t        numbers = [f.get(k) for k in ('value', 'low', 'high') if isinstance(f.get(k), (int, float))]\n   387\t        if any(not math.isfinite(n) or abs(n) > LIMIT for n in numbers):\n   388\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a number that is not finite or beyond {LIMIT:g}'))\n   389\t            continue\n   390\t        has_value = isinstance(f.get('value'), (int, float))\n   391\t        has_low, has_high = isinstance(f.get('low'), (int, float)), isinstance(f.get('high'), (int, float))\n   392\t        if has_value == (has_low or has_high) or has_low != has_high:\n   393\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure has value, or low and high, never both and never one end alone'))\n   394\t        elif has_low and has_high and f['low'] > f['high']:\n   395\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: low is above high'))\n   396\t        kind = f['kind']\n   397\t        if kind != 'source' and (f.get('evidence') or f.get('quote_number') or (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none'):\n   398\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\n   399\t        if kind != 'computed' and (f.get('inputs') or f.get('operation')):\n   400\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs and operation are for computed figures only'))\n   401\t        if kind != 'estimate' and f.get('rests_on'):\n   402\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on is for estimates only'))\n   403\t        if kind == 'source':\n   404\t            if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   405\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source cannot have basis forecast'))\n   406\t            if not f.get('evidence') or not f.get('quote_number'):\n   407\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source needs evidence_id and quote_number'))\n   408\t        elif kind == 'computed':\n   409\t            ins, op = f.get('inputs') or [], f.get('operation')\n   410\t            if op not in ARITY or not ins:\n   411\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a computed figure needs inputs and an operation from the closed set'))\n   412\t            else:\n   413\t                lo_n, hi_n = ARITY[op]\n   414\t                if len(ins) < lo_n or (hi_n is not None and len(ins) > hi_n):\n   415\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: {op} takes ' + (f'exactly {lo_n}' if hi_n == lo_n else f'{lo_n} or more') + f' inputs, not {len(ins)}'))\n   416\t            for i in ins:\n   417\t                if i in passages and i not in figs:\n   418\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs name passage {i}; inputs name figures only'))\n   419\t                elif i not in figs:\n   420\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: input {i} is not a figure'))\n   421\t        elif not _text(f.get('basis_text')):\n   422\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: an estimate needs basis_text'))\n   423\t        for r in f.get('rests_on') or []:\n   424\t            if r not in figs and r not in passages:\n   425\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on names {r}, which is neither a figure nor a shown passage'))\n   426\t    state = {}\n   427\t\n   428\t    def cyclic(fid):\n   429\t        if state.get(fid) == 1:\nP-v4-claude.claude.json\nP-v4-claude.codex.json\nP-v4-claude.md\ncodex.stream\nprompt.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/small_cases.py.diff | sed -n '1,200p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4_census.py | sed -n '1,195p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t--- small_cases.py\t2026-11-22 19:43:03\n     2\t+++ /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/s4tree/small_cases.py\t2026-11-22 20:11:04\n     3\t@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@\n     4\t from pathlib import Path\n     5\t \n     6\t HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n     7\t sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))\n     8\t sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / 'src'))\n     9\t if os.environ.get('CALC_SMALL_DIR'):                     # small_mutants.py: a mutated calc_small.py on a scratch copy\n    10\t@@ -356,6 +356,26 @@\n    11\t def _():\n    12\t     a = base_pair()\n    13\t     a['figures'].append(F('REST', W('other', 'FY2027'), 'computed', 'forecast', 'USD bn', value=30.375, inputs=['F6', 'F12'], operation='difference'))\n    14\t+    return a, PAIR\n    15\t+\n    16\t+\n    17\t+@case('A1', 'a guidance-implied growth rate applied to the Q3 forecast for Q4 (D741 ruling 1) [D742]', 'pass')\n    18\t+def _():\n    19\t+    a = base_pair()\n    20\t+    a['figures'].append(F('G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='revenue sequential growth'), 'computed', 'guidance', '%', value=12.27, inputs=['F2', 'F3'], operation='growth'))\n    21\t+    fig(a, 'F5').update(kind='computed', basis_text=None, rests_on=None, inputs=['F4', 'G'], operation='apply_growth', value=123.49)\n    22\t+    fig(a, 'F6').update(value=411.29)\n    23\t+    fig(a, 'F11').update(value=114.23)\n    24\t+    fig(a, 'F12').update(value=380.28)\n    25\t+    return a, PAIR\n    26\t+\n    27\t+\n    28\t+@case('A1', 'the Data Center Q3 forecast grown at the guided company rate for Q4 (D741 ruling 1) [D742]', 'pass')\n    29\t+def _():\n    30\t+    a = base_pair()\n    31\t+    a['figures'].append(F('G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='revenue sequential growth'), 'computed', 'guidance', '%', value=12.27, inputs=['F2', 'F3'], operation='growth'))\n    32\t+    fig(a, 'F11').update(inputs=['F10', 'G'], operation='apply_growth', value=114.23)\n    33\t+    fig(a, 'F12').update(value=380.28)\n    34\t     return a, PAIR\n    35\t \n    36\t \n    37\t@@ -391,6 +411,20 @@\n    38\t defect('an unused second forecast of a quarter is not counted (passes)', 'pass', lambda a: a['figures'].append(F(\n    39\t     'ALT', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3'), 'estimate', 'forecast', 'USD bn', value=112.0, basis_text='an alternative not used')))\n    40\t defect('Q3 guidance used directly beside the Q3 forecast (S4)', 'S4', lambda a: fig(a, 'F10').update(inputs=['F3', 'F9'], value=99.9))\n    41\t+defect('the guided Q2-to-Q3 rate applied to Q2 rebuilds a Q3 value beside the forecast (S4) [D742]', 'S4', lambda a: (a['figures'].extend([\n    42\t+    F('G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='revenue sequential growth'), 'computed', 'guidance', '%', value=12.27, inputs=['F2', 'F3'], operation='growth'),\n    43\t+    F('Q3B', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3'), 'computed', 'guidance', 'USD bn', value=108.0, inputs=['F2', 'G'], operation='apply_growth')]),\n    44\t+    fig(a, 'F10').update(inputs=['Q3B', 'F9'], value=99.9), fig(a, 'F12').update(value=370.575)))\n    45\t+MESSAGES['the guided Q2-to-Q3 rate applied to Q2 rebuilds a Q3 value beside the forecast (S4) [D742]'] = 'Q3B for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3'\n    46\t+defect('Q4 built on the guided Q3 through the guided multiple applied twice to Q2 (S4) [D742]', 'S4', lambda a: (a['figures'].append(\n    47\t+    F('X', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='revenue sequential multiple'), 'computed', 'guidance', 'x', value=1.1227, inputs=['F3', 'F2'], operation='ratio')),\n    48\t+    fig(a, 'F5').update(kind='computed', basis_text=None, rests_on=None, inputs=['F2', 'X', 'X'], operation='product', value=121.25),\n    49\t+    fig(a, 'F6').update(value=409.05), fig(a, 'F11').update(value=112.16), fig(a, 'F12').update(value=378.21)))\n    50\t+defect('Q4 grown from a run-rate rebuilt from Q2 at the guided rate, not from the Q3 forecast (S4) [D742]', 'S4', lambda a: (a['figures'].extend([\n    51\t+    F('G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='revenue sequential growth'), 'computed', 'guidance', '%', value=12.27, inputs=['F2', 'F3'], operation='growth'),\n    52\t+    F('T', W('company', 'FY2027 Q3', measure='revenue run-rate'), 'computed', 'guidance', 'USD bn', value=108.0, inputs=['F2', 'G'], operation='apply_growth')]),\n    53\t+    fig(a, 'F5').update(kind='computed', basis_text=None, rests_on=None, inputs=['T', 'G'], operation='apply_growth', value=121.25),\n    54\t+    fig(a, 'F6').update(value=409.05), fig(a, 'F11').update(value=112.16), fig(a, 'F12').update(value=378.21)))\n    55\t defect('two Data Center Q4 forecasts, both used (S4)', 'S4', lambda a: (a['figures'].append(F(\n    56\t     'BAD', W('Data Center', 'FY2027 Q4'), 'estimate', 'forecast', 'USD bn', value=104.0, basis_text='another view')),\n    57\t     fig(a, 'F12').update(outcome_for=[]), a['figures'].append(F('X', W('Data Center', 'FY2027'), 'estimate', 'forecast', 'USD bn', value=372.0,\n     1\t\"\"\"Census: every stored v13s first reply (both blind tests) through the current checker and the rate-stop copy; print any change.\"\"\"\n     2\timport importlib.util, json, sys\n     3\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     4\tR = Path('/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers')\n     5\tsys.path.insert(0, str(R)); sys.path.insert(0, '/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src')\n     6\timport calc_small as cur\n     7\tspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('calc_fix', sys.argv[1]); fix = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(fix)\n     8\timport blind_test as bt\n     9\tfrom hivemind import model\n    10\tjudged = changed = 0\n    11\tfor folder in ('judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22', 'judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22'):\n    12\t    out = Path('/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data') / folder\n    13\t    for name in bt.CASES:\n    14\t        it = json.loads((out / name / 'item.json').read_text())\n    15\t        for w in bt.WRITERS:\n    16\t            data = bt.first_json((out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').read_text())\n    17\t            if model.check_schema(data, it['schema']):\n    18\t                print(folder, name, w, 'schema error'); continue\n    19\t            res = {}\n    20\t            for label, mod in (('cur', cur), ('fix', fix)):\n    21\t                calc = mod.from_answer(data, it['family'])\n    22\t                r = mod.check(it['family'], it['passages'], calc)\n    23\t                res[label] = (sorted(map(tuple, r['errors'])), mod.publish_decision(it['family'], calc, r))\n    24\t            judged += 1\n    25\t            s4 = [e for e in res['cur'][0] if e[0] == 'S4']\n    26\t            flag = 'CHANGED' if res['cur'] != res['fix'] else 'same'\n    27\t            changed += flag == 'CHANGED'\n    28\t            print(f'{folder[-13:]:13} {name:5} {w:7} {flag:7} cur={res[\"cur\"][1]} fix={res[\"fix\"][1]} S4cur={s4} errs_fix={res[\"fix\"][0]}'[:400])\n    29\tprint(f'judged {judged}, changed {changed}')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
