gment Q4 directly'\\\\'',a)\nestimated_rate(a,'\\\\''G'\\\\'','\\\\''F11'\\\\'','\\\\''ER'\\\\'',rate,sc.W('\\\\''Data Center'\\\\'','\\\\''FY2027 Q4'\\\\'','\\\\''revenue sequential growth'\\\\''),'\\\\''F10'\\\\'','\\\\''I assume segment growth tracks the company guidance-implied pace.'\\\\'')\njoint('\\\\''joint: company guidance informs estimated segment pace'\\\\'',a)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"saved refused [('S4', 'v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8')] {'v4': [416.6193762993763, 416.6193762993763, None]}\nQ3 unlisted, same arithmetic publish [] {'v4': [416.6193762993763, 416.6193762993763, None]}\nthird-party Q3, direct rate refused [('S4', 'v4: uses F7 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8')] {'v4': [423.16823284823283, 423.16823284823283, None]}\nthird-party Q3, estimated benchmark publish [] {'v4': [423.16823284823283, 423.16823284823283, None]}\nsaved figures, explicit Q4 estimated growth publish [] {'v4': [416.6193762993763, 416.6193762993763, None]}\nsaved figures, explicit Q4 dollar estimate publish [] {'v4': [416.62, 416.62, None]}\njoint: company guidance feeds segment Q4 directly refused [('S4', 'v8: uses F3 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F4')] {'v4': [402.8, 402.8, None], 'v8': [380.28076923076924, 380.28076923076924, None]}\njoint: company guidance informs estimated segment pace publish [] {'v4': [402.8, 402.8, None], 'v8': [380.28076923076924, 380.28076923076924, None]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt | sed -n '629,652p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r56b.md | sed -n '1,39p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '27,48p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt | sed -n '629,652p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r56b.md | sed -n '1,39p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '27,48p'\"","aggregated_output":"   629\t        key = _key(want)\n   630\t        node = by_key.get(key) or nodes.get(key, [])\n   631\t        for v, out in sorted(outcomes.items()):\n   632\t            reached, stack, seen_ids = [], [out], set()\n   633\t            while stack:\n   634\t                fid = stack.pop()\n   635\t                if fid in seen_ids or fid not in figs:\n   636\t                    continue\n   637\t                seen_ids.add(fid)\n   638\t                f = figs[fid]\n   639\t                if _key(f['what']) == key:\n   640\t                    reached.append(fid)\n   641\t                    if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   642\t                        continue                           # through the forecast figure: what lies beneath it is its basis\n   643\t                if f['unit'] in ('%', 'x'):\n   644\t                    continue                               # a rate or multiple is another quantity: what it is computed from supplies no value of this one\n   645\t                stack.extend(f.get('inputs') or [])\n   646\t            if not reached:\n   647\t                continue\n   648\t            if len(node) != 1:\n   649\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n   650\t                continue\n   651\t            bypass = sorted(r for r in reached if r != node[0])      # the walk never descends below the forecast figure\n   652\t            if bypass:\n     1\t**Do not build the narrowed change.** It publishes an answer that counts **$112.5B** for Q3 in the annual sum, then resets Q4’s starting amount to the **$108B** Q3 guidance. The current checker refuses that answer.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — The growth-rate exemption still lets a second quarterly value into the outcome.** I changed the saved P-v4 reply to start Q4 at `$112.5B × (1 + growth($112.5B, $108B))`, then grow that starting amount by 12%. The first step gives exactly $108B. The narrowed checker publishes **$411.28B** because it sees the adopted forecast in the base and skips the rate that cancels it. The same probe with the quoted guidance range publishes **$408.86B–$413.70B**. When I put the guidance directly in the base calculation as a dollar deduction, the narrowed checker still refused it. In a separate joint-answer probe, a Q4 Data Center base inherited the $110B company Q3 forecast through Q4, and a rate built from Q3 reset it to the $108B guidance basis. That also published. [Checker diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/calc_small.py.diff:22) · [Arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:230) · [Saved P-v4 answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/claude.json:1) · [Joint fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:80)\n     4\t\n     5\t   **Minor — `rests_on` is an existing reader case.** I put the same substitution in a Q4 estimate that explicitly relied on the $108B guidance as its starting amount. Both versions published and recorded the competing Q3 figures. The check for two adopted forecasts follows `rests_on`. The check for bypassing a listed forecast follows arithmetic inputs. D738 assigns the use of a number inside an estimate to readers. [D738](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11258) · [Adopted forecasts](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:594) · [Reader records](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:732)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Serious — The code is both wider and narrower than Alex’s answer.** Alex approved using guidance to inform a 13% Q4 forecast when $113.6B supplies the only Q3 dollar amount. I encoded that example as a 13% estimate resting on the guidance-implied rate. Both versions published. The narrowed code also published when the rate used the outside $113.6B estimate in place of guidance. In the other direction, it refused the saved answer when I wrote the same Q4 calculation as `$112.5B × ($108B / $96.2B)`. The recomputed annual outcome was still **$416.62B**. It also refused the new Data Center example when I expressed the same $101.75B starting amount as an estimate resting on the shared company forecast and the mix. The rulings record puts the meaning of the benchmark in the request and reader brief. [Alex’s exact ruling](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11346) · [Outside estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:258) · [Checker diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/calc_small.py.diff:22)\n     8\t\n     9\t3. **Wording — State the dollar-value rule consistently.** The schema says *guidance*, while the code skips any qualifying percentage. The header calls it the “one exception” and says “built on,” which leaves writers guessing whether an estimate resting on the forecast qualifies. Both reader versions preserve the single-dollar-value condition, but mentioning `apply_growth` invites readers to treat the operation as proof. The system’s “run-rate that carries the guided growth forward” leaves all of those readings open. Use the same plain rule across all four places: guidance can set the pace, and the adopted forecast supplies the quarter’s dollar value. Tell readers to look through the rate for a second dollar value. Any restriction on how writers must express the arithmetic belongs in the request. [System text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Schema and header diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/render_v13.py.diff:10) · [Reader diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/blind_test.py.diff:10) · [Adjudicator diff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/blind_adjudicate.py.diff:3)\n    10\t\n    11\t4. **Minor — Both new mutants are decisive, and one of the three refusal cases overlaps an existing check.** Both new passing cases fail with the exemption disabled. I also tried requiring the forecast as the immediate base. Only the Data Center case failed, so it earns its place. The Q2-built run-rate is the only case that catches removal of the base condition. The repeated-multiple case catches a return to the blanket cutoff. The explicitly labelled rebuilt Q3 still refuses under the current, blanket, narrowed and no-base versions because the walk sees the rebuilt Q3 figure before reaching its rate. It adds a computed-figure example to the existing direct-bypass check. All 109 cases and all 42 mutants reproduced in memory. The stored-reply replay also reproduced one change in 24, the saved P-v4 refusal. The decisive missing pair is the saved valid answer and the guidance-reset version that this round showed can publish beside it. [New cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/small_cases.py.diff:17) · [Existing bypass case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:388) · [New mutants](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/r56b/small_mutants.py.diff:10) · [Stored-reply census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4_census.py:1)\n    12\t\n    13\t**Verdict — Do not build.**    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md | sed -n '37,65p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py | sed -n '71,112p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.codex.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.claude.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md | sed -n '37,65p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py | sed -n '71,112p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.codex.json; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.claude.json\"","aggregated_output":"    37\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`reader-packets/`, `adjudication/`, `TALLY.json`)\n    38\t\n    39\tEach reply had two blind readers, a Claude subagent and a Codex session. Each saw the whole request and the whole reply, and nothing\n    40\tof the writer or the checker. Where the two disagreed, two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point against the passages and\n    41\tthe format rules. A point counts as settled when both adjudicators agree.\n    42\t\n    43\t**Settled defects on accepted replies.**\n    44\t- **R06 (P-v4, Codex writer), two forecasts of one quarter.** Both readers and both adjudicators marked it.\n    45\t  - F7 = growth(Q2, F4) takes Q3 from the $108B guidance outside the Q3 forecast F6 ($113.6B). It reaches the outcome through an\n    46\t    estimate's `rests_on` (F8, which rests on F7), so S4's walk, which follows `inputs` only, did not see it. Under D738 this is an\n    47\t    indirect form, recorded for readers.\n    48\t  - The effect is about $6B on Q4, and the rung is unchanged.\n    49\t\n    50\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    51\t- every \"number misread\" claim. These were a \"plus or minus\" expression given as its midpoint, which the format permits; \"up to\",\n    52\t  which in e1919's quote belongs to the $3B total and not to the $1B tranche; and \"about $70 billion\", which was kept;\n    53\t- both \"wrong arithmetic\" claims:\n    54\t  - R09 declared the midpoint of a computed range, which the format permits;\n    55\t  - R02 summed \"more than $42 billion\" and $25 billion as 67, keeping the qualifier on its source;\n    56\t- R12's two-forecast claim (guidance used as a benchmark beneath an estimate, with one Q3 value in the outcome chain).\n    57\t\n    58\t**Unsettled: the two families split** (the Codex adjudicator upholds, the Claude adjudicator rejects).\n    59\t- **A computed market reference.**\n    60\t  - The cases:\n    61\t    - consensus company revenue ($411B) × the reported Q2 Data Center share (92.5%) gives a Data Center reference of about $380B\n    62\t      (R05, R08, R12);\n    63\t    - the reported $25B balance − the $18B \"committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year\" gives $7B (R02).\n    64\t  - **The Codex adjudicator's view:** applying a quarter's share to a full year, or netting the schedule, is the writer's assumption,\n    65\t    so it is not the market's reference.\n    71\tdef build(folder, packet_ids):\n    72\t    key = json.loads((folder / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n    73\t    out = folder / 'adjudication'\n    74\t    out.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n    75\t    for pid in packet_ids:\n    76\t        case, writer = key[pid]['case'], key[pid]['writer']\n    77\t        codex = json.loads((folder / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.codex.json').read_text())\n    78\t        claude = json.loads((folder / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.claude.json').read_text())\n    79\t        schema = json.loads((folder / case / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    80\t        text = '\\n'.join([HEADER, '# The two readings', *reading_lines('Reader 1', codex), *reading_lines('Reader 2', claude), '',\n    81\t                          '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    82\t                          json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    83\t                          '# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)', (folder / case / 'user.txt').read_text(), '',\n    84\t                          '# The answer', (folder / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json').read_text()])\n    85\t        target = out / f'{pid}.md'\n    86\t        if target.exists():\n    87\t            raise SystemExit(f'{target} exists')\n    88\t        target.write_text(text)\n    89\t        (out / f'{pid}.prompt.txt').write_text(f'Read the file {target} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow its '\n    90\t                                               'instructions and return only the JSON it asks for.\\n')\n    91\t        print(f'{pid}: {case}/{writer}, {len(text)} chars')\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n    95\t    if len(sys.argv) < 3:\n    96\t        raise SystemExit(__doc__)\n    97\t    build(Path(sys.argv[1]).resolve(), sys.argv[2:])\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"refusals\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"message\": \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\",\n     5\t      \"stated\": \"yes\",\n     6\t      \"sentence\": \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\",\n     7\t      \"right\": \"yes\",\n     8\t      \"why\": \"The calculation is analytically sound. The $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint and $96.2 billion Q2 actual imply 12.27% guided sequential growth. Applying that rate to the sole Q3 forecast of $112.5 billion gives about $126.3 billion for Q4. The $112.5 billion estimate reflects past guidance beats and current supply constraints. Adding both quarters to $177.82 billion of reported first-half revenue gives about $416.6 billion, within rung 2. The guidance midpoint enters the calculation separately to set the growth rate, so it bypasses F8 in the calculation chain while Q3 revenue is counted once.\"\n     9\t    }\n    10\t  ]\n    11\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"refusals\": [\n     3\t  {\n     4\t   \"message\": \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\",\n     5\t   \"stated\": \"yes\",\n     6\t   \"sentence\": \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\",\n     7\t   \"right\": \"yes\",\n     8\t   \"why\": \"F4 ($108bn guidance) never enters the total as a second Q3 level; it builds F9, the guided Q2-to-Q3 growth (12.27%, matching the passages' 12%); Q4 = F8 x 1.1227. The system text asks guidance to give 'a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward'. An analyst would accept it. Caveat: the schema allows a guidance-derived rate 'as a stated benchmark for an estimate', but F10 is computed, so the rule's wording catches this path while the run-rate instruction pulls the other way.\"\n     9\t  }\n    10\t ],\n    11\t \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the reviewer hand-back; marks and sentence verbatim; why shortened.\"\n    12\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
